{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfdg6JvhKFgpzQcdUMczW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the 8 test statistics to `iter_3/critique/test_stats/`. Each one runs on the observed responses (7,680 trials, 118 participants), returns a finite float, and takes under 0.1 s per call. I did not run the harness; the pipeline does that and decides which discrepancies are significant.\n\nI chose them using the earlier rounds' results. Participants' left/right bias came out significant in iterations 0 and 1, and the incumbent model has no term for it. The spread in how often people agree with the majority was significant in iteration 2, and this refinement was written to fix that.\n\n| Statistic | What it probes | Reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| `participant_side_bias_variance` | Personal button bias, which the model doesn't include | obs > null means real people have side biases |\n| `participant_majority_agreement_variance` | Whether the per-person antisymmetry weight fixed last round's discrepancy | obs > null means it is still under-dispersed |\n| `split_half_agreement_correlation` | Whether agreeing with the majority is a stable personal trait (odd vs even trials) | obs > null means it is more trait-like than the model implies |\n| `side_bias_vs_agreement_correlation` | Whether disengaged participants press one button, when the model's guesses are side-neutral coin flips | obs < null means they press one button |\n| `late_minus_early_alternation_preference` | Drift over the session in preferring the more alternating sequence; the model's judgement doesn't change | obs > null means people drift toward alternation |\n| `participant_alternation_preference_variance` | How much people differ in preferring alternation, against the model's spread of personal ideal rates | obs > null means preferences are more polarised |\n| `half_balance_choice` | Heads/tails bunching into one half of a sequence, beyo